<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Federico II Gonzaga, Duke of Mantua</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1500">1500</a>–<a href="/years/1540">1540</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-gonzaga">House of Gonzaga</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Federico II Gonzaga, son of Isabella d'Este, ruled Mantua from 1519 and was raised from marquis to duke by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> in 1530. Through his marriage to Margherita Paleologa he acquired the Marquisate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montferrat">Montferrat</a> in 1536. A great patron, he commissioned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giulio-romano">Giulio Romano</a> to build and decorate the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palazzo-te">Palazzo Te</a>, and collected paintings by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/titian">Titian</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonio-da-correggio">Correggio</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/federico-ii-gonzaga-duke-of-mantua.jpg" alt="Federico II Gonzaga, Duke of Mantua" width="320" height="406" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Titian · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tiziano_-_Federico_II_Gonzaga_(1529)_2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Federico was the son of Francesco II Gonzaga, Marquess of Mantua, and Isabella d'Este. Because of the turbulent politics of the time, he spent three years from the age of ten as a hostage in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-julius-ii">Pope Julius II</a>, and from 1515 to 1517 he was the hostage of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-i-of-france">Francis I of France</a>, to ensure Gonzaga help in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. In these situations he served as a diplomatic representative of his family and gained courtly experience. In Rome he was tutored by Ausonio and acquired a taste for festivals and parties.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>On 3 April 1519 Federico succeeded his father as Marquis of Mantua, at first under the regency of his mother and his uncles Sigismondo and Giovanni Gonzaga. He received imperial investiture from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> on 7 April 1521. In July 1521 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-leo-x">Pope Leo X</a> named him Captain General of the Church, and he fought the French at Parma in 1521 and at Piacenza in 1522.</p><p>His marriages served his wish to gain <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montferrat">Montferrat</a>. He signed a contract with Maria Palaeologina, heir to the marquisate, but in 1528 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-clement-vii">Pope Clement VII</a> voided it in exchange for two prisoners. He then contracted to marry Julia of Aragon, and in 1530 was granted the ducal title. When Boniface, Marquis of Montferrat, died after a fall from a horse, Federico paid Charles 50,000 ducats to annul the Aragon contract and pressed the pope to restore the earlier agreement. After Maria's death he married her sister Margaret on 3 October 1531. When the last legitimate male Palaiologos heir died, Montferrat passed to the Gonzaga, who held it until the 18th century.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Like his parents, Federico was a patron of the arts, and his years in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> left him an admirer of Roman art. He sent agents to Rome and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> to buy works, and in 1527, during the sack of Rome, wrote to the imperial commander Fabrizio Maramaldo asking whether looted statues or busts were for sale. He acquired several works by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonio-da-correggio">Correggio</a>, bought and commissioned paintings from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/titian">Titian</a>, and had his portrait painted by Titian and by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raphael">Raphael</a>. Giulio Romano spent sixteen years as his court artist. A notarial contract of 1535 shows Flemish paintings entering <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> through his court.</p><p>Federico suffered long from syphilis, like his father, and died in 1540 at his villa at Marmirolo. His son Francesco briefly held the title of duke before dying in his teens, and the second son, Guglielmo, became the third duke and Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montferrat">Montferrat</a> and continued the line.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Federico II Gonzaga of Mantua” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Gonzaga ruled as imperial vicars, became marquises in 1433 under Gianfrancesco Gonzaga and dukes in 1530 under <b>Federico II</b>, who also acquired Montferrat, later the Duchy of Montferrat.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-mantua">Duchy of Mantua</a></li><li><blockquote>After the last Palaiologos died in 1533, Charles V awarded Montferrat to <b>Federico II Gonzaga</b> of Mantua in 1536.</blockquote><a href="/state/march-of-montferrat">March of Montferrat</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Mantua. Died in Marmirolo, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=45.22,10.76&amp;year=1526" aria-label="Marmirolo on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4806 568 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Federico II Gonzaga, Duke of Mantua"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5256 838h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5256 838h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=45.22,10.76&amp;year=1526">What was here in 1526? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
